## R7.6 / R7.7 / R7.8 — error stream, flush-before-exit, JSON error schema
421:**R7.6 — Error messages. (MUST / SHOULD)**
422-Errors **MUST** be written to stderr (R7.1) and **SHOULD** be lowercase-first, free of a trailing period, name the offending input in quotes, and state a next step where possible. When a machine output format is active, errors follow the machine-readable error contract in R7.8.
423-✅ `error: project 'demo' not found — run 'acme project list' to see available projects`
424-❌ `ERROR!! Something went wrong.`
425-
426:**R7.7 — Buffering & flush. (SHOULD / MUST)**
427-Because stdout is fully buffered when it is not a terminal (ISO C), diagnostics on stderr **SHOULD** be line- or unbuffered so they appear promptly, and a tool **MUST** flush all streams before exiting on every path — success, error, and signal (R9.6) — so no output is lost. Tools **SHOULD NOT** rely on the interleaved ordering of stdout and stderr, since buffering makes that ordering non-deterministic between a TTY and a pipe.
428-✅ flushing stdout before `exit(0)`; the final JSON line is never dropped on a panic
429-❌ losing the last line of output because the buffer wasn't flushed before exit
430-
431:**R7.8 — Machine-readable error schema. (MUST)**
432-Under any machine output format (`-o json`/`--json`, `-o jsonl`, `-o yaml`, or `-o name`), an ordinary command error **MUST** be emitted to stderr as a single JSON object: `{"error":{"code":"<stable-string>","message":"<human-readable>"}}` — a JSONL stream **MUST NOT** suppress or replace it (see below). `code` **MUST** be a stable, documented string, and the object **MAY** carry `details`, `request_id`, or other documented fields. JSONL/NDJSON **MAY** be used for documented streaming or multi-record machine output, where each line is one complete JSON object, but it **MUST NOT** replace the single-object error schema for ordinary command failures, including a failure that occurs mid-stream. If a parser can determine that a machine format was requested before reporting a usage error, it **SHOULD** use the same machine-readable error contract; otherwise it **MAY** fall back to the standard human usage error format.
433-✅ `{"error":{"code":"not_found","message":"project 'demo' not found"}}` ❌ a bare unparseable string under `-o json`; emitting JSONL for a one-error command failure
434-
435-**R7.9 — Bare & unknown invocation. (MUST / SHOULD)**
436-The bare top-level command with no arguments **MUST** print help to stdout and exit `0`. A command *group* invoked with no action (`acme project`) is a usage error: print help to stderr and exit `2`. On an unknown command or flag, the tool **SHOULD** suggest the nearest match.
437-✅ `acme` → help/stdout/0; `acme projcet list` → "did you mean 'project'?" ❌ `acme project` silently doing nothing and exiting `0`
438-
439-**R7.10 — Watch / follow streams. (SHOULD)**

## R9.6 — signals
502:**R9.6 — Signals. (MUST)**
503-A tool **MUST** exit cleanly on `SIGINT` with code `130`, releasing resources, and **MUST** handle `SIGPIPE`/`EPIPE` gracefully — when a reader like `head` or `less` closes the pipe, exit quietly without a broken-pipe stack trace. It **MUST** also treat `SIGTERM` like `SIGINT` — clean up, flush (R7.7), and exit `143` (128+15) — so it behaves correctly under containers, orchestrators, and CI runners.
504-✅ `acme widget list | head -5` ends silently; `kill <pid>` shuts down cleanly with code `143` ❌ a Python traceback on `BrokenPipeError`; ignoring SIGTERM until killed with `SIGKILL`
505-
506-**R9.7 — Telemetry & update checks. (MUST / SHOULD)**
507-Telemetry **MAY** be on by default only when it is prominently documented and disclosed, minimizes collected data, and is easy to disable. A CLI with telemetry **MUST** document what is collected, why it is collected, whether it is anonymous/pseudonymous/identified, and all disable mechanisms. It **MUST** support `TOOL_TELEMETRY=0`/`false`, **SHOULD** honor `DO_NOT_TRACK=true`, and **SHOULD** provide a persistent config control such as `acme config set telemetry disabled`. Telemetry **MUST NOT** transmit secrets, full argv, argument values, environment values, file contents, request/response bodies, or resource payloads unless a separate explicit opt-in covers that data. Telemetry and update-availability checks **MUST NOT** run in non-interactive/CI contexts by default. Update checks, if present, **SHOULD** be non-blocking, cached, and disableable. A tool **MAY** offer self-update via `acme upgrade`.
508-
509-**R9.8 — Empty-state & first-run. (SHOULD)**
510-On first run or empty results, a tool **SHOULD** guide the next step rather than printing nothing or a bare error.
511-✅ `no projects yet — create one with 'acme project create <name>'`
512-❌ an empty line, or a stack trace, when there is simply nothing to show

## R8.2 — non-interactive exit mapping (quoted above in full at line 461)
**R8.2 — Non-interactive / CI behavior. (MUST)**
A tool **MUST** be fully usable without a TTY. It **MUST NOT** block on a prompt when input is unavailable; instead it uses provided flags/env or fails with a clear message. Use exit `2` when required non-auth input or consent is missing because prompting is disabled/unavailable, use exit `4` when authentication or authorization input is missing, and use exit `1` for runtime input failures such as unreadable files. `--no-input` forces this mode explicitly.

